\textbf{Why is this a contribution and not just a tuning note?}
Differentiable budget prediction is a recurring design pattern in
adaptive compression (AdaViT~\cite{meng2022adavit},
DynamicViT~\cite{rao2021dynamicvit}) and the collapse failure mode is
independent of the specific predictor architecture: it arises whenever
the budget loss is the only counterweight to an unbounded VQA gradient
acting through a monotone-in-$k$ mask. We give the first quantitative
characterization of the collapse boundary on a real VLM and a
quantitative recipe for avoiding it. Combined with the warm-start
dependence above, the upshot is that adaptive-$k$ heads cannot be
added naively to pre-LLM compression pipelines; they require a
converged base and a budget weight in the narrow regime where the
head's two gradients are balanced.

